---
import ConceptLayout from '../layouts/ConceptLayout.astro';
---

<ConceptLayout
  title="OpenVidya"
  summary="Interactive physics documentation where the model, the code, the verification and the limits of validity are all part of the material."
>
  <p>
    Two prototype slices, chosen to be structurally as different as possible.
    They exist to answer one question before any general architecture is
    designed: <b>what does a physics concept module actually need to declare?</b>
  </p>

  <div class="cards">
    <a href="/mechanics/projectile-motion" class="card">
      <span class="tag">dynamical system · ODE</span>
      <h3>Projectile Motion</h3>
      <p>A trajectory through time. Initial conditions, numerical integration, closed-form validation.</p>
    </a>
    <a href="/fields/electric-field" class="card">
      <span class="tag">field · electrostatics</span>
      <h3>Electric Field of Point Charges</h3>
      <p>A vector at every point of space. No initial conditions, no time evolution, validated by Gauss's law.</p>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>The working principle</h2>
  <blockquote>
    Humans define the physical model and the educational intent. Agents implement
    and challenge it. Executable tests verify what can be verified. Humans judge
    what cannot. Git records the resulting knowledge.
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    One distinction does most of the work here. A test that recomputes what the
    implementation already computed proves only that the code agrees with itself.
    Every check in these two modules is derived by a <b>different route</b> from
    the implementation — closed-form solutions against numerical integration,
    Gauss's law against pointwise superposition. That is why the checks carry
    information.
  </p>

  <p>
    The thing tests <em>cannot</em> establish is whether the chosen model is the
    right one for a given situation, or whether the animation builds the correct
    mental picture. Those remain human judgments, and the material says so
    openly rather than hiding it.
  </p>

  <style>
    .cards { display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); margin: 2rem 0; }
    .card { display: block; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius: 12px; padding: 1.25rem; text-decoration: none; color: inherit; transition: border-color .15s, transform .15s; }
    .card:hover { border-color: #1d4ed8; transform: translateY(-2px); }
    .card h3 { margin: .5rem 0 .4rem; }
    .card p { margin: 0; color: #6b7280; font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.5; }
    .tag { font-size: .7rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .08em; color: #1d4ed8; }
  </style>
</ConceptLayout>
